            <ArticleTitle>Assessing health-related quality of life among coronary patients: SF-36 vs SF-12.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>615-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.puhe.2009.07.013</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">to compare health-related quality of life (HRQL) assessed using the Short Form-12 (SF-12) and the Short Form-36 (SF-36) questionnaires in coronary patients with different diagnoses, testing the hypothesis that the SF-12 is capable of discriminating between patients with acute myocardial infarction (AMI) and patients with unstable angina in the same way as the SF-36.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cross-sectional study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">HRQL was studied in 186 patients admitted to hospital for ischaemic cardiopathy, using the SF-36 and SF-12. Intraclass correlation coefficients were calculated for each summary component. The proportion of variability of the physical and mental summary components of the SF-36 (PCS-36 and MCS-36) explained by each component of the SF-12 was examined using a linear regression model, adjusted for age and gender.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The mean scores observed were similar in the two questionnaires. The degree of agreement was high, and the corresponding regression model explained 87% of the variability in the PCS-36 and 93% of the variability in the MCS-36. The SF-12 identified the differences between AMI and angina in the same way as the SF-36.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The SF-12 replicates the information of the summary component scores of the SF-36, and discriminates between patients with AMI and those with unstable angina. Its use allows the same information to be obtained as from the SF-36, with less effort for the patient and the doctor.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
